body:
Found while doing the #3795 follow-up sweep on the sixth alias pair (`top`/`limit`). The `filter` → `where` fold that #3795 settled at the protocol layer exists at the **engine** layer too — in exactly one of six methods.

`ObjectQL.find()` folds it, with a comment saying why:

```ts
// packages/objectql/src/engine.ts (find)
// Normalize the `filter` alias → `where`. The DataEngine contract
// (`spec/data/data-engine.zod.ts`) exposes both, but the driver AST only
// understands `where`; an internal caller passing `{ filter }` would
// otherwise match ALL rows (silent over-grant — surfaced by ADR-0057's
// sharing/graph read path). `where` wins when both are present.
if ((ast as any).filter != null && ast.where == null) { ast.where = (ast as any).filter; }
delete (ast as any).filter;
```

`findOne`, `count`, `update`, `delete` and `aggregate` take the same option bags and have **no such fold**. The consequence the comment describes ("would otherwise match ALL rows") is therefore live in all five — and on the write methods it is not an over-grant, it is an unbounded write.

## Measured, not inferred

A real `ObjectQL` engine + in-memory driver, three rows (`a: open`, `b: done`, `c: done`), each call passing only `filter`:

| call | result | correct |
|---|---|---|
| `find({filter: {status:'done'}})` | `[b, c]` | ✅ the one method that folds |
| `findOne({filter: {status:'done'}})` | **`a`** | `b` — returned a row that does not match |
| `count({filter: {status:'done'}})` | **3** | 2 |
| `update({title:'ZAP'}, {filter:{status:'done'}, multi:true})` | **`a`, `b`, `c` all rewritten** | only `b`, `c` |
| `delete({filter:{status:'done'}, multi:true})` | **table emptied** | only `b`, `c` deleted |

The write methods reach `driver.updateMany(object, ast, …)` / `driver.deleteMany(object, ast)` with `ast.where === undefined`, which every driver reads as "no predicate".

## Why this is reachable, not theoretical

`ScopedContext` — the cross-object API handed to L2 hook bodies — forwards its argument verbatim:

```ts
// packages/objectql/src/engine.ts (ScopedContext)
async findOne(query: any = {}) { return this.engine.findOne(this.objectName, { ...query, context: this.context }); }
async delete(options: any = {}) { return this.engine.delete(this.objectName, { ...options, context: this.context }); }
```

and the spec's own hook documentation teaches the broken call:

```ts
// packages/spec/src/data/hook.zod.ts:403
 *   const users = ctx.api.object('user');
 *   const admin = await users.findOne({ filter: { role: 'admin' } });
```

So a hook author following the documented example gets an arbitrary user rather than the admin — and `users.delete({ filter: …, multi: true })` in a cleanup hook empties the object. `options` is typed `any` on every facade method, so nothing catches it at compile time. The deprecated `DataEngine{Update,Delete,Count,Query}OptionsSchema` in the spec also still declare `filter` as a legitimate option for these operations, so "the caller shouldn't pass it" is not a defensible position — the contract says they may.

## Suggested fix

The #3795 machinery already exists and is exported: `RPC_QUERY_ALIAS_SLOTS` + `foldQueryAliasSlots` (`@objectstack/spec/data`). Route every engine entry point through one call to it instead of adding four more open-coded copies of `find`'s guard — the point of #3795 was to decide precedence once, and the engine is the third reader that sweep did not reach.

1. Fold `filter` → `where` (canonical wins; different values are a conflict, per #4181) in `findOne`, `count`, `update`, `delete`, `aggregate`, and replace `find`'s hand-rolled copy with the same call.
2. Same pass, the sixth alias pair: `top` → `limit` is folded **unguarded** in `protocol.ts` (`options.limit = Number(options.top)` — the alias overwrites the canonical key) and **guarded** in `engine.find` (`top != null && limit == null` — canonical wins). `limit` is canonical (`top` is described as "Alias for limit (OData compatibility)" in both `QuerySchema` and `EngineQueryOptionsSchema`), so the protocol has it backwards, and `{top: 1, limit: 3}` resolves to 1 through HTTP and 3 through a direct engine call — the #3795 divergence on the one pair its scope note excluded.
3. Fix the `hook.zod.ts` example to `where` regardless of which way (1) lands: it is a `.describe()`-adjacent doc block that ships to `content/docs/references/`.
4. Pin each method: `{filter}` alone applies the predicate; `{filter, where}` conflicting is refused; `{top}` alone still limits; `{top, limit}` conflicting is refused.

Note the write-path pins matter most — a regression here is silent and destructive, and the current test suite has none (this whole class went unnoticed because `find` was the only method anyone thought to check).

Refs #3795, #4181, #3713, ADR-0057.
